---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadIdentities } from "../lib/identities.mjs";
import "@fontsource/inter/latin-400.css";
import "@fontsource/inter/latin-500.css";
import "@fontsource/inter/latin-600.css";
import "@fontsource/inter/latin-700.css";
import "@fontsource/plus-jakarta-sans/latin-600.css";
import "@fontsource/plus-jakarta-sans/latin-700.css";
import "@fontsource/plus-jakarta-sans/latin-800.css";
import "../styles/site.css";

const identities = loadIdentities();
const identityLink = (id) => `<a href="/identities/${id.slug}/">${id.slug}</a>`;
const identityLinks = (lane) => identities.filter((id) => id.lane === lane).map(identityLink).join(", ");
const catalogueCount = `${identities.length} ${identities.length === 1 ? "identity" : "identities"}`;
---
<Base title="About - uiforagents" description="From a vanilla UI kit to design identities for agent-built apps: the pivot, the licenses, and what ships next.">
  <div class="container">
    <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom: 24px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>From vanilla kit to design identities</h1>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight">
      <div class="about-copy">
        <p>
          uiforagents started as an agent-first vanilla UI kit: copy-in HTML/CSS/JS components with
          zero runtime dependencies, themed by a visitor-facing direction switcher. It proved the
          core idea - agents can ship production UI when the design decisions are made for them.
        </p>
        <p>
          But "components" was the wrong unit. A pile of well-styled parts still leaves every
          product decision open: which font, how dense, what radius, when to move. Agents don't need
          more parts; they need a point of view. So the kit became a catalogue
          of <strong>design identities</strong> - complete, opinionated systems built on shadcn/ui,
          Tailwind v4, and Base UI, each shipped with theme tokens, component overrides, composed
          blocks, fonts, motion defaults, and an agent prompt-pack.
        </p>
        <p>
          The workflow got simpler too: install an identity into any shadcn app with one command,
          hand your agent the prompt-pack, and ship. No design deliberation, no drift between
          pages - the identity is the contract.
        </p>
        <p>
          And the catalogue is not a fixed menu - uiforagents is a <strong>design system
          factory</strong>. When no identity fits, your agent derives a new system from the nearest
          one: retune the palette, type, radius, and density; inherit everything structural; and
          ship a design system that is coherent because it inherited a tested one. Derived systems
          live in your project, and the ones that recur can be promoted into the catalogue. The
          full contract is the <a href="/derive/">derive guide</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          What changed, in one line: the vanilla kit content was dropped in October 2026 - it lives on
          only in git history, in
          the <a href="https://github.com/peteretelej/uiforagents">uiforagents</a> repo and this repo's history.
        </p>

        <h2 id="license">License</h2>
        <p>
          uiforagents is <a href="https://github.com/peteretelej/uiforagents">Apache-2.0</a>. Upstream
          projects flow through with their own notices: shadcn/ui (MIT), Tailwind CSS (MIT), and
          Base UI (MIT). Identities may also name their fonts - install them
          via <code>@fontsource</code> packages under their own licenses.
        </p>

        <h2>Roadmap</h2>
        <p>
          Shipped: all {catalogueCount} in the catalogue - the shadcn/ui
          kits <Fragment set:html={identityLinks("react")} />, and the pure-CSS artifact
          identities <Fragment set:html={identityLinks("artifact")} /> - each with its theme or
          foundation stylesheet, blocks, agent prompt-pack, and per-identity tuning-surface
          notes. Derivation is a documented contract too: the <a href="/derive/">derive guide</a>
          and every pack's tuning surface tell your agent exactly which knobs to retune. Next:
          more identities across new moods and surfaces, and tighter agent integration as the
          registry ecosystem grows. Identities continue the queued-card pattern on
          the <a href="/#identities">landing gallery</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
